<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements of the release build (scripts/release.sh), signed with the Hardened Runtime.

  Not sandboxed, on purpose (see docs/DISTRIBUTION.md, "Por que sem App Sandbox"):
  scheduled jobs install launch agents in ~/Library/LaunchAgents and call launchctl; SQLite files are opened
  anywhere and SQLite writes -wal/-shm/-journal siblings next to them; SSH tunnels read private keys and
  known_hosts under ~/.ssh; PostgreSQL/MySQL/Redis connect over Unix sockets in /tmp and /var/run; export
  folders and backups are remembered across launches. None of that works sandboxed without security-scoped
  bookmarks for every path plus a privileged helper for launchd.

  The two sandbox keys below only take effect if com.apple.security.app-sandbox is ever turned on; they are
  listed so the intent (network client, user-chosen files) stays explicit. No Hardened Runtime exceptions are
  needed: no JIT, no unsigned executable memory, no DYLD variables, and every loaded library (Sparkle) is
  signed with the same identity, so library validation stays on.
  No keychain-access-groups: passwords live in the login keychain (file-based), whose access is decided by
  each item's ACL (the app's designated requirement), not by an entitlement. That key would also require an
  embedded provisioning profile for a Developer ID app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
